ignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.704,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-7-20-6"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"7020006","sanskritFile":"Ram07.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"0567b4d83387660852d810e4c4d18cb2e8131dc38a6388a77ed07df1df0936b9","compact":"d2482382c4a155a87394b5ee83dde617e21354cdceefa5e5f08dc10a04f173ef","nasal":"d2482382c4a155a87394b5ee83dde617e21354cdceefa5e5f08dc10a04f173ef"}},{"id":"VRCE-7-20-7","kanda":7,"kandaId":"uttara","sarga":20,"verse":7,"english":"O mighty-armed one, o lord of Rākşasas, behold man, albeit having their sense stupified, engaged in the pursuit of various interests, and themselves not understanding their own ways;* some pass their time merrily with dancing and playing on musical instruments while others weep distressfully with tears streaming from their eyes down their cheeks. *Gati, according to the commentator, means the time of employment and suffering. I think the ordinary meaning answers, and give it accordingly.","alignment":{"type":"grouped","confidence":0.79,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-7-20-11"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"7020007","sanskritFile":"Ram07.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"3b3b9b05e1137de8afbbd97bfeafcb51167b195fe6a350c1813485f6c777f033","compact":"35b54d314ee46b1b194b97ee4643250a872ffb368d6560f3726c1c24c88eeca1","nasal":"35b54d314ee46b1b194b97ee4643250a872ffb368d6560f3726c1c24c88eeca1"}},{"id":"VRCE-7-20-8","kanda":7,"kandaId":"uttara","sarga":20,"verse":8,"english":"O mighty-armed one, o lord of Rākşasas, behold man, albeit having their sense stupified, engaged in the pursuit of various interests, and themselves not understanding their own ways;* some pass their time merrily with dancing and playing on musical instruments while others weep distressfully with tears streaming from their eyes down their cheeks. *Gati, according to the commentator, means the time of employment and suffering. I think the ordinary meaning answers, and give it accordingly.","alignment":{"type":"grouped","confidence":0.625,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-7-20-11"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"7020008","sanskritFile":"Ram07.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"63d22c5dcbb504a6b9659794e7aedea94214892dfcc87de93ae73a898009b1da","compact":"0c9a8cb618628c7ea4c48d391a18045d51b17ae1ae2e44fd06b7a6fcfacfc974","nasal":"1ad1f290cace9db2b3904892b7c7b98953a2a715b2744023b75481ccae8aba6d"}},{"id":"VRCE-7-20-9","kanda":7,"kandaId":"uttara","sarga":20,"verse":9,"english":"Down fallen on account of their attachment for their mothers and fathers and sons, and of their desires touching their wives and friends they set small store by labours having the hereafter as their object.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.632,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-7-20-12"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"7020009","sanskritFile":"Ram07.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"d188886210549ad630ad73a8f6c2af0c3492fdc508ac942fd236a70531081263","compact":"d5a86d33b6ad16b00e0c570c1e47f6ef1b958903d0f46b2fa0cd7b72dbf9110f","nasal":"a943aa902e4e821ebab7d081d763411722e5bdae1ab4f2e8ec086443aa987252"}},{"id":"VRCE-7-20-10","kanda":7,"kandaId":"uttara","sarga":20,"verse":10,"english":"What then is the use of affecting a race that is brought down ever by its own infatuation. O placid one, this world is verily conquered by you, there is no doubt.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":1.0,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-7-20-13"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"7020010","sanskritFile":"Ram07.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"e7a23ad460a872eda31e71ed56e0bb421f2963dcb7c84e497320bfbd768aebcd","compact":"a72565d601457bd574449